{"record_id": "entry-5656", "entry_number": 5656, "status": "needs_context", "title": "Senate Acquits Trump", "description_short": "Senate Acquits Trump", "description_medium": "The Senate acquitted Trump on both articles in his first impeachment trial, largely along party lines. Mitt Romney was the only Republican senator to vote for conviction. The outcome prevented removal through that trial, but an acquittal vote should be distinguished from a finding that every allegation was disproved or that later political consequences were inevitable.", "description_long": "The Republican-led Senate acquitted Trump on both articles in his first impeachment trial. Voting largely followed party lines, with Senator Mitt Romney the sole Republican to vote for conviction. The result meant Trump was not removed from office through that trial. The impeachment process and its voting outcome should be described separately from conclusions about the strength of every allegation. An acquittal vote does not, by itself, demonstrate that all disputed conduct was disproved, just as a partisan division does not establish every senator's private motivation. The event marked the Senate's resolution of the first trial rather than the end of every political or legal question surrounding Trump's conduct. The supported account preserves the two acquittals and Romney's distinctive vote while keeping later interpretations separate from the documented decision.", "category": "Government Corruption", "subcategory": null, "topic_tags": [], "people_tags": ["Donald Trump", "Mitt Romney"], "organization_tags": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1162/vote_116_2_00034.htm"], "preserved_fields": ["title", "description_short"], "notes": ["M", "i", "s", "s", "i", "n", "g", " ", "d", "e", "s", "c", "r", "i", "p", "t", "i", "o", "n", "s", " ", "c", "o", "m", "p", "o", "s", "e", "d", " ", "f", "r", "o", "m", " ", "s", "u", "p", "p", "l", "i", "e", "d", " ", "e", "v",